# The NEGATIVE controls for .github/scripts/assert_provider_linked.sh (#46).
#
# REAL, UNMODIFIED DuckLake builds that genuinely have no KMS provider linked
# into them. The gate must go RED on these, and until it has been watched do so
# its green on a healthy artifact says nothing.
#
# Why published bytes rather than a fixture manufactured on the spot: the
# obvious plant is to take the good artifact and delete the provider records
# with `objcopy --strip-symbols`. That is CIRCULAR - it manufactures the fixture
# by deleting exactly the table the gate reads, so the refusal is guaranteed by
# construction and proves nothing about the gate. It is also not even a
# provider-less binary: objcopy rewrites .symtab and leaves .dynsym alone, so
# the "stripped" library still exports the provider and would still register
# the factory at load. Measured on the artifact of run 32756499818:
#     .dynsym  21 GLOBAL DEF + 7 WEAK DEF provider symbols
#     .symtab  the same 28, plus 20 LOCAL DEF
# Stripping .symtab removes 48 records and changes nothing about what the
# library does.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRIMARY control - ELF, linux_amd64. MUST match the format this workflow
# publishes. An earlier cut of this file used only the PE control below, and
# "one tool, two artifacts, opposite verdicts" was then false: the two artifacts
# did not share a format, so the real tier never demonstrated the gate returns
# zero on a provider-less ELF.
#
# These are DuckDB's own upstream ducklake bytes for the pinned duckdb release.
# Nobody here built them, and they predate this fork's KMS overlay entirely, so
# the absence of the provider is a property of the artifact rather than of how
# the fixture was prepared.
#
#   $ curl -sSL -o x.gz http://extensions.duckdb.org/v1.5.5/linux_amd64/ducklake.duckdb_extension.gz
#   $ gunzip x.gz && file x
#   x: ELF 64-bit LSB shared object, x86-64, BuildID[sha1]=26ac1aba..., not stripped
#   $ .github/scripts/assert_provider_linked.sh --expect present x
#       sha256      e51bf9e8d933d0e83780ae096455501b542cf962569a2ce5613532d702c08302
#       symbols     53872
#       anchor(DuckLakeCatalog) 182
#       provider    0 (defined + EXTERNAL only)
#   exit 1
#
# Measured 2026-08-24 on the CURRENT cut of the gate (external-linkage filter
# in force), with llvm-nm on macOS; the same file is re-measured on GNU nm in
# CI on every run, which is what the pipeline actually depends on.
#
# 53872 symbols and 182 anchor sightings are what make the 0 mean something:
# the symbol table is demonstrably real and demonstrably a DuckLake one, so the
# zero is an absent provider and not a misaimed tool.
ELF_CONTROL_URL=http://extensions.duckdb.org/v1.5.5/linux_amd64/ducklake.duckdb_extension.gz
ELF_CONTROL_SHA256=e51bf9e8d933d0e83780ae096455501b542cf962569a2ce5613532d702c08302
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECONDARY control - PE32+ mingw DLL, DuckDB's own upstream ducklake build for
# the same pinned duckdb release as the ELF control above. Kept for FORMAT
# BREADTH only: it shows the gate is not accidentally ELF-specific. It is NOT
# the primary control, because reading it needs a `nm` with the pe-x86-64 BFD
# target, which stock ubuntu:22.04 does NOT have - there `nm` says "file format
# not recognized" and the gate refuses with "not a symbol table". That fails
# CLOSED, which is correct, but it diagnoses the artifact when the fault is the
# toolchain. The workflow therefore installs binutils-mingw-w64-x86-64 and
# asserts nm can read this file BEFORE the gate runs, so a missing toolchain
# reports as a missing toolchain.
#
# It used to be an asset of THIS repository's own v0.2.0-rc.2 release, fetched
# with `gh release download`. That is no longer sourceable: the release assets
# are gone, and a release that already exists with zero assets cannot have them
# restored in place, so the fetch printed "no assets to download" and the PE
# half of the control proved NOTHING - precisely the class of green the control
# exists to refuse. A repository's own mutable release assets are the wrong
# anchor for a fixture; the upstream versioned extension path is immutable and
# is where the ELF control already comes from.
#
# Synthesising a minimal PE here instead was considered and rejected for the
# same reason the objcopy plant above is rejected. The gate requires >=1000
# symbols and a sighting of the DuckLakeCatalog anchor before it will return a
# verdict at all, so a hand-made object would be refused as "not a symbol
# table" - a refusal that says nothing whatever about the provider path this
# control is aimed at.
#
# Measured 2026-09-12 by walking the COFF symbol table of the pinned bytes
# directly: 202971 symbols, 468 sightings of DuckLakeCatalog, 44744 DEFINED +
# EXTERNAL symbols, of which 0 match the provider patterns - so the gate must
# refuse it with "0 provider symbols", and the zero is an absent provider
# rather than a misaimed tool. Re-measured on GNU nm in CI on every run, which
# is what the pipeline actually depends on.
#
#   $ curl -sSL -o x.gz http://extensions.duckdb.org/v1.5.5/windows_amd64_mingw/ducklake.duckdb_extension.gz
#   $ gunzip x.gz && file x
#   x: PE32+ executable (DLL) (console) x86-64, for MS Windows
PE_CONTROL_URL=http://extensions.duckdb.org/v1.5.5/windows_amd64_mingw/ducklake.duckdb_extension.gz
PE_CONTROL_SHA256=7d457487d9364163a30577aec8999750a770c6fd9082709fa0648ef7e8c3f249
